WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.428 --> 00:00:15.260
Recently,

00:00:15.284 --> 00:00:19.083
I've been declaring
to anyone who would listen

00:00:19.107 --> 00:00:22.467
that I am a dangerous woman.

00:00:22.491 --> 00:00:26.196
(Applause)

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.864
Now, declaring that boldly like this

00:00:28.888 --> 00:00:32.245
still feels a bit dangerous,

00:00:32.269 --> 00:00:34.945
but it also feels right.

00:00:35.352 --> 00:00:37.173
At this time in my life,

00:00:37.197 --> 00:00:39.818
about to be 77,

00:00:39.842 --> 00:00:41.003
I have --

00:00:41.027 --> 00:00:42.036
(Applause)

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.111
I love when you're applauded
for your age --

00:00:44.135 --> 00:00:45.160
(Laughter)

00:00:45.184 --> 00:00:46.342
but I'll take it.

00:00:46.366 --> 00:00:48.812
(Applause)

00:00:50.931 --> 00:00:56.040
About to be 77, I realize
that I have nothing left to prove,

00:00:56.064 --> 00:00:57.584
less to lose,

00:00:57.608 --> 00:01:01.405
and I'm more impatient about everything.

00:01:01.992 --> 00:01:05.948
The true, slow pace towards equality,

00:01:05.972 --> 00:01:08.654
the rise in sexism, racism,

00:01:08.678 --> 00:01:11.099
violence against women and girls ...

00:01:11.623 --> 00:01:13.926
And I'm angry, too,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:17.401
at the climate deniers
who are stealing the future

00:01:17.425 --> 00:01:20.118
from our children and grandchildren.

00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:24.835
Friends, we are living in dangerous times.

00:01:25.553 --> 00:01:30.685
And such times call for all of us
to be more dangerous.

00:01:31.202 --> 00:01:33.408
Now, what do I mean by this?

00:01:33.432 --> 00:01:35.209
I don't mean being feared.

00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:37.654
It's not that kind of dangerous.

00:01:37.678 --> 00:01:41.802
But I do mean being more fearless.

00:01:42.397 --> 00:01:44.736
I mean speaking the truth

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.593
when silence is a lot safer.

00:01:47.617 --> 00:01:51.747
I mean speaking up in rooms
for those who aren't present,

00:01:51.771 --> 00:01:54.361
especially those rooms
where decisions are made

00:01:54.385 --> 00:01:56.903
about our lives and our bodies.

00:01:57.359 --> 00:02:00.502
We need to be in those rooms,

00:02:01.288 --> 00:02:04.547
showing up for one another,

00:02:04.571 --> 00:02:07.299
challenging the cultural construct

00:02:07.323 --> 00:02:10.860
that encourages us,
especially women and girls,

00:02:10.884 --> 00:02:13.270
to compete, compare,

00:02:14.214 --> 00:02:15.560
criticize.

00:02:16.365 --> 00:02:18.582
We have to end this.

00:02:19.547 --> 00:02:20.977
And speaking out

00:02:21.001 --> 00:02:23.144
against the policies and the politics

00:02:23.168 --> 00:02:27.358
that divide us and diminish
our collective power

00:02:27.382 --> 00:02:31.174
as a global community of women,

00:02:31.198 --> 00:02:33.264
and the men and the allies

00:02:33.288 --> 00:02:34.828
who stand with us.

00:02:35.637 --> 00:02:40.679
Becoming dangerous also means
embracing whatever risks are necessary

00:02:40.703 --> 00:02:44.647
to create a world
where women and girls are safe

00:02:44.671 --> 00:02:47.147
in their homes and at work,

00:02:47.171 --> 00:02:49.855
where all voices are represented

00:02:49.879 --> 00:02:51.580
and respected,

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:54.362
all votes counted,

00:02:54.386 --> 00:02:56.490
the planet protected.

00:02:57.363 --> 00:02:59.273
And this is all possible.

00:02:59.969 --> 00:03:02.032
Because we're ready for this.

00:03:02.525 --> 00:03:06.787
We're better prepared
than any generation ever before us,

00:03:06.811 --> 00:03:08.908
better resourced, better connected.

00:03:08.932 --> 00:03:12.351
In many parts of the world,
we're living longer than ever.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:19.422
Women over 65 are among
the fastest-growing populations on earth,

00:03:19.446 --> 00:03:23.227
with the potential for becoming
the most powerful, too.

00:03:24.021 --> 00:03:25.214
Now --

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:27.982
(Applause)

00:03:28.006 --> 00:03:30.481
What a change this represents.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:35.735
Postmenopausal women like me,
not that long ago,

00:03:35.759 --> 00:03:37.540
were considered useless

00:03:37.564 --> 00:03:38.912
or crazy.

00:03:39.872 --> 00:03:43.169
We were valued for caregiving
and grandmothering --

00:03:43.193 --> 00:03:45.749
and I really love that part.

00:03:46.662 --> 00:03:48.066
But we were pushed aside

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:52.048
and expected to retire
to our rocking chairs.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.543
Women on the dangerous side of 60

00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:57.394
are not retiring.

00:03:57.418 --> 00:04:00.082
We are rewiring --

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:03.063
(Applause)

00:04:04.595 --> 00:04:09.274
taking all that we know and have done --
and that is a lot --

00:04:09.298 --> 00:04:12.151
to redefine what age looks like,

00:04:12.175 --> 00:04:15.146
can do, can accomplish.

00:04:15.756 --> 00:04:19.803
But becoming dangerous isn't about
becoming a certain age,

00:04:19.827 --> 00:04:22.271
because at each end of the age spectrum,

00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:25.614
brave women and girls are stepping up,

00:04:25.638 --> 00:04:29.377
taking the risk to create change.

00:04:30.584 --> 00:04:34.121
I became a risk-taker
early in my life's journey.

00:04:34.145 --> 00:04:35.447
I had to,

00:04:35.471 --> 00:04:38.696
or have my life defined by the limitations

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.568
for a girl growing up in the rural South,

00:04:41.592 --> 00:04:44.048
with no money, no connections,

00:04:44.072 --> 00:04:46.199
no influence.

00:04:46.748 --> 00:04:48.897
But what wasn't limited

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:52.931
was my curiosity about the world
beyond my small town,

00:04:52.955 --> 00:04:57.205
beyond the small minds
of a still-segregated South,

00:04:57.229 --> 00:04:59.843
a world that I glimpsed in the newsreels

00:04:59.867 --> 00:05:02.821
at the one movie theater in town,

00:05:02.845 --> 00:05:05.299
and a world that got a lot closer to me

00:05:05.323 --> 00:05:11.187
when I met Miss Shirley Rountree,
my eighth-grade English teacher.

00:05:12.171 --> 00:05:14.382
From the minute she walked
into the classroom,

00:05:14.406 --> 00:05:16.350
her high heels clicking,

00:05:16.374 --> 00:05:18.936
she was a woman in charge,

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.621
with perfect hair, signature red lips,

00:05:21.645 --> 00:05:23.803
colorfully coordinated, head to toe.

00:05:23.827 --> 00:05:26.503
I wanted to be her.

00:05:27.381 --> 00:05:33.929
Gratefully, she became my first mentor
and helped me become me.

00:05:34.811 --> 00:05:38.034
With her support, I got
a scholarship to college --

00:05:38.058 --> 00:05:39.918
the first in my family --

00:05:39.942 --> 00:05:42.401
and landed at a big state university,

00:05:42.425 --> 00:05:47.274
right in the middle of two
great social justice movements:

00:05:47.298 --> 00:05:49.949
civil rights for African Americans

00:05:49.973 --> 00:05:52.304
and equal rights for women.

00:05:52.858 --> 00:05:56.481
I joined both with enthusiasm,

00:05:56.505 --> 00:06:00.358
only to discover that my newfound activism

00:06:00.382 --> 00:06:03.370
and my fermenting feminism

00:06:03.394 --> 00:06:09.541
would often be in direct conflict
with my deeply embedded need to please

00:06:10.467 --> 00:06:11.850
and be popular.

00:06:12.617 --> 00:06:16.418
In my first job as a college teacher,
I broke the rules,

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:20.217
and I encouraged students
to join me in the protest marches.

00:06:20.241 --> 00:06:22.640
And when I found out
that my male colleague

00:06:22.664 --> 00:06:25.822
with the same experience and education

00:06:25.846 --> 00:06:28.334
was being paid more than me,

00:06:28.358 --> 00:06:30.559
I mounted a personal protest.

00:06:31.688 --> 00:06:34.021
When my raise was denied,

00:06:34.045 --> 00:06:38.072
with the excuse that
he had a family to support,

00:06:38.096 --> 00:06:41.235
so did I as a single mom.

00:06:41.861 --> 00:06:45.793
But I dropped my protest to keep my job.

00:06:46.508 --> 00:06:49.959
Today, millions of women
are making this compromise,

00:06:49.983 --> 00:06:53.811
staying in their jobs without equal pay

00:06:53.835 --> 00:06:55.558
for equal work.

00:06:56.709 --> 00:07:00.419
And as one of the first women
on television in the '70s,

00:07:00.443 --> 00:07:03.221
I was warned that focusing
on women's stories

00:07:03.245 --> 00:07:05.204
would limit my career opportunities,

00:07:05.228 --> 00:07:07.031
and maybe it did.

00:07:07.055 --> 00:07:12.574
But I got to produce and host
breakthrough programming for women,

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.541
while at the same time,

00:07:14.565 --> 00:07:16.509
remaining silent

00:07:16.533 --> 00:07:19.379
about sexual harassment

00:07:19.403 --> 00:07:23.290
and listening to consultants
who were hired to advise me

00:07:23.314 --> 00:07:25.046
about my appearance.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:26.883
"Become a blonde."

00:07:26.907 --> 00:07:28.068
I did.

00:07:28.092 --> 00:07:29.694
"Lower your voice."

00:07:29.718 --> 00:07:30.946
I tried.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:32.934
"Lower your necklines."

00:07:32.958 --> 00:07:34.163
I didn't.

00:07:34.187 --> 00:07:35.368
(Laughter)

00:07:35.392 --> 00:07:38.564
But I did wear those ugly anchor suits

00:07:38.588 --> 00:07:43.036
with those scarves that look
something like men's ties.

00:07:43.864 --> 00:07:46.446
And later, in the power
positions in media,

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.921
often as the first or only woman,

00:07:48.945 --> 00:07:52.733
aware of being judged
through that gender lens,

00:07:52.757 --> 00:07:55.297
I struggled from time to time

00:07:55.321 --> 00:08:00.508
to find the right balance between
being a leader for women

00:08:00.532 --> 00:08:05.947
and not being entirely defined
as a woman leader.

00:08:06.523 --> 00:08:11.031
But today, I'm proud to be known
as a woman leader.

00:08:11.055 --> 00:08:14.431
(Applause)

00:08:17.407 --> 00:08:21.795
As an activist, advocate, feminist

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:26.725
and as a newly declared dangerous woman,

00:08:26.749 --> 00:08:29.965
I'm caring less what others say

00:08:29.989 --> 00:08:34.154
and saying more clearly
what I think and feel.

00:08:34.178 --> 00:08:35.688
And let me be clear:

00:08:36.355 --> 00:08:41.476
I acknowledge my privilege
in being able to do that,

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:44.443
to speak my truth.

00:08:47.379 --> 00:08:49.043
And to stand here today

00:08:49.067 --> 00:08:51.225
with this opportunity

00:08:51.249 --> 00:08:54.659
to talk to you about women and power --

00:08:54.683 --> 00:08:58.808
note I did not say "empowered."

00:08:58.832 --> 00:09:01.949
I don't think we're waiting
to be empowered.

00:09:01.973 --> 00:09:04.187
I think we have power.

00:09:04.211 --> 00:09:06.946
(Applause)

00:09:08.317 --> 00:09:14.798
What we need are more opportunities
to claim it, to use it,

00:09:14.822 --> 00:09:16.099
to share it.

00:09:16.123 --> 00:09:17.733
And yes, I know --

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:21.285
there are women with power
who don't use it well or wisely

00:09:21.309 --> 00:09:23.555
and who don't share it.

00:09:24.372 --> 00:09:26.780
I've heard, as I'm sure you have,

00:09:26.804 --> 00:09:28.667
those stories that begin with,

00:09:28.691 --> 00:09:32.254
"The worst boss I ever had
was a woman ... "

00:09:32.278 --> 00:09:36.904
And we could all name women leaders
who have not made us proud.

00:09:36.928 --> 00:09:40.168
But we can change all of that

00:09:40.192 --> 00:09:43.787
with a simple but brilliant idea

00:09:43.811 --> 00:09:49.075
that I first heard from a risk-taking,
dangerous congresswoman from New York

00:09:49.099 --> 00:09:50.813
named Bella Abzug.

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.993
Bella said, "In the 21st century,

00:09:55.017 --> 00:09:58.844
women will change the nature of power

00:09:58.868 --> 00:10:03.289
rather than power changing
the nature of women."

00:10:04.175 --> 00:10:06.094
From the moment I heard that --

00:10:06.118 --> 00:10:07.874
(Applause)

00:10:07.898 --> 00:10:10.949
I thought, "This is our call to action.

00:10:10.973 --> 00:10:13.890
This is our biggest opportunity."

00:10:14.871 --> 00:10:16.792
And as a journalist and an activist,

00:10:16.816 --> 00:10:20.027
I've seen this idea in action,

00:10:20.051 --> 00:10:25.287
documenting the stories of women
on both sides in long-term conflicts,

00:10:25.311 --> 00:10:29.287
coming together
and defying the official power

00:10:29.311 --> 00:10:36.244
to form alliances and find their own ways
to ending violence in their communities.

00:10:36.921 --> 00:10:39.785
And as an activist,
I've traveled to places

00:10:39.809 --> 00:10:43.488
where it's dangerous to be born a woman,

00:10:43.512 --> 00:10:45.565
like eastern Congo,

00:10:45.589 --> 00:10:49.803
where a war is being waged
on the bodies of women.

00:10:50.528 --> 00:10:54.764
There, at a healing and leadership
center called City of Joy,

00:10:54.788 --> 00:11:00.185
brave Congolese women
are transforming pain into power

00:11:00.209 --> 00:11:03.849
by training survivors of sexual assault

00:11:03.873 --> 00:11:07.037
to return to their villages as leaders.

00:11:07.868 --> 00:11:10.004
And at recent climate summits,

00:11:10.028 --> 00:11:14.151
I've observed women climate leaders
working behinds the scenes,

00:11:14.175 --> 00:11:16.307
out of the public spotlight,

00:11:16.331 --> 00:11:21.228
making sure that the negotiations
toward global climate agreement

00:11:21.252 --> 00:11:23.450
continue to move forward.

00:11:23.474 --> 00:11:26.407
So as we move forward
in our lives and work

00:11:26.431 --> 00:11:28.854
and we have more power and influence,

00:11:28.878 --> 00:11:31.375
let's change the nature of power

00:11:31.399 --> 00:11:36.911
by dismantling some of the barriers
that remain for those who follow

00:11:36.935 --> 00:11:39.275
by advocating and agitating

00:11:39.299 --> 00:11:43.754
for fairer and truer
and more equal representation

00:11:43.778 --> 00:11:47.156
in every room and at every table.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:49.463
Now, be warned:

00:11:50.447 --> 00:11:52.891
if you advocate for a woman

00:11:52.915 --> 00:11:55.942
for an open position or promotion,

00:11:55.966 --> 00:11:58.439
you could be challenged with,

00:11:58.463 --> 00:12:01.455
"You're playing the women's card"

00:12:01.479 --> 00:12:03.919
or "the race card"

00:12:03.943 --> 00:12:06.728
if advocating for a woman of color.

00:12:07.419 --> 00:12:10.565
I've had this experience,
as I'm sure you have.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:15.730
"Are you running an affirmative
action program here at PBS?"

00:12:15.754 --> 00:12:19.316
asked one of my board members
when, as a new president,

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:23.951
I announced my first hires
as five qualified women.

00:12:23.975 --> 00:12:26.503
Now, my affirmative action had been

00:12:26.527 --> 00:12:30.398
to ask that the search firm
bring me a candidate list

00:12:30.422 --> 00:12:33.431
that included the names of women
and people of color

00:12:33.455 --> 00:12:36.155
who just happened to be, in my judgment,

00:12:36.179 --> 00:12:40.231
the best candidates
for the position as well.

00:12:41.632 --> 00:12:44.194
I say, dangerous women

00:12:44.218 --> 00:12:46.250
and our allies:

00:12:46.274 --> 00:12:49.758
it's time to play the women's card,

00:12:49.782 --> 00:12:50.941
play the race card,

00:12:50.965 --> 00:12:52.782
play all our cards.

00:12:52.806 --> 00:12:56.148
(Applause)

00:13:00.552 --> 00:13:03.902
Not to win the power game,

00:13:03.926 --> 00:13:06.601
but to lead to better outcomes

00:13:06.625 --> 00:13:07.951
for everyone.

00:13:08.596 --> 00:13:10.194
And it's time, too,

00:13:10.218 --> 00:13:13.443
to discard that scarcity theory,

00:13:13.467 --> 00:13:14.867
the one that says,

00:13:14.891 --> 00:13:18.189
there's only room
for one of us at the top,

00:13:18.213 --> 00:13:20.949
so protect your turf,

00:13:20.973 --> 00:13:23.600
don't make friends or allies.

00:13:24.519 --> 00:13:26.512
Changing the nature of power

00:13:26.536 --> 00:13:30.245
transforms "protect your turf"
into "share your turf,"

00:13:30.269 --> 00:13:31.937
it encourages coalitions,

00:13:31.961 --> 00:13:33.539
it builds alliances,

00:13:33.563 --> 00:13:36.952
it strengthens and sustains friendships.

00:13:37.948 --> 00:13:43.411
My women friends
are my source of renewable energy.

00:13:43.982 --> 00:13:47.223
(Applause)

00:13:49.064 --> 00:13:52.340
So are my mentors, my champions,

00:13:52.364 --> 00:13:55.020
my supporters, my sponsors,

00:13:55.044 --> 00:14:00.511
and all of the ways that we can
and do show up for each other.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:07.994
We can become our sources
of renewable power

00:14:08.018 --> 00:14:09.311
for each other.

00:14:10.168 --> 00:14:11.589
And along the way,

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:14.760
we need to take better care of ourselves,

00:14:14.784 --> 00:14:17.456
and here, I am not the best role model.

00:14:18.019 --> 00:14:19.379
I don't meditate.

00:14:19.837 --> 00:14:22.155
I don't exercise regularly.

00:14:22.996 --> 00:14:26.368
But I do live aerobically.

00:14:26.715 --> 00:14:29.553
(Laughter)

00:14:30.225 --> 00:14:33.032
(Applause)

00:14:34.776 --> 00:14:38.993
Because I believe we can't be dangerous

00:14:39.017 --> 00:14:40.374
from the sidelines,

00:14:41.239 --> 00:14:44.899
and there's just too much to be done.

00:14:46.121 --> 00:14:47.937
So let's use all our power.

00:14:47.961 --> 00:14:49.715
How about the power of money?

00:14:50.334 --> 00:14:54.318
Let's allocate more
of our philanthropic dollars,

00:14:54.342 --> 00:14:56.147
our campaign donations,

00:14:56.171 --> 00:14:58.233
our investment funds,

00:14:58.257 --> 00:15:02.159
to increase economic and political equity.

00:15:02.872 --> 00:15:06.359
And let's leverage the power
of media and technology

00:15:06.383 --> 00:15:10.013
that we have
in our hands, quite literally,

00:15:10.037 --> 00:15:14.751
to elevate each other's stories and ideas;

00:15:15.504 --> 00:15:17.617
to practice civility;

00:15:17.641 --> 00:15:19.187
to seek the truth,

00:15:19.988 --> 00:15:21.781
which is diminishing

00:15:21.805 --> 00:15:25.734
and is threatening
free and open societies.

00:15:26.535 --> 00:15:33.155
Yes, we have all that we need
to move our communities forward.

00:15:34.257 --> 00:15:36.824
And the best thing we have,

00:15:36.848 --> 00:15:39.140
and what we must remember,

00:15:39.164 --> 00:15:41.321
is to be there for each other.

00:15:42.196 --> 00:15:45.040
We will move forward together,

00:15:45.064 --> 00:15:48.092
willing now to take more risk,

00:15:48.116 --> 00:15:50.665
to be more fearless,

00:15:50.689 --> 00:15:53.655
to speak up, speak out

00:15:53.679 --> 00:15:55.449
and show up

00:15:55.473 --> 00:15:56.841
for one another.

00:15:57.782 --> 00:16:00.225
George Bernard Shaw once wrote

00:16:00.249 --> 00:16:05.734
that he believed in his opinion
that his life belonged to the community,

00:16:05.758 --> 00:16:08.856
that the harder he worked,
the more he lived

00:16:08.880 --> 00:16:12.803
and that he wanted to be
thoroughly used up when he died.

00:16:13.509 --> 00:16:15.200
He went on to write,

00:16:15.224 --> 00:16:18.804
"Life is no brief candle to me

00:16:18.828 --> 00:16:21.022
but a splendid torch

00:16:21.046 --> 00:16:23.831
that I have got hold of for a moment

00:16:23.855 --> 00:16:27.668
before passing to future generations."

00:16:28.532 --> 00:16:33.002
I, too, do not view my life
as a brief candle,

00:16:33.026 --> 00:16:35.781
although I am burning it at both ends.

00:16:35.805 --> 00:16:37.130
(Laughter)

00:16:37.154 --> 00:16:40.469
And I do want it, and me,

00:16:40.493 --> 00:16:45.192
to be thoroughly used up when I die.

00:16:46.231 --> 00:16:49.166
But at this point in my life's journey,

00:16:49.967 --> 00:16:52.543
I am not passing my torch.

00:16:53.247 --> 00:16:57.532
I am holding it higher than ever,

00:16:57.556 --> 00:17:00.090
boldly, brilliantly,

00:17:00.981 --> 00:17:06.916
inviting you to join me
in its dangerous light.

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:08.876
Thank you.

00:17:08.900 --> 00:17:11.534
(Applause)

